Why Your RAG System Returns Outdated Answers (And How to Fix It)
When a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system ignores newly indexed documents, the breakdown typically occurs in one of four areas within your AI pipeline:
1. Caching Overrides
Semantic or API caching layers may be intercepting the query and serving a previously generated, obsolete response before the retriever even runs.
2. Ranking Conflicts
Vector similarity prioritizes semantic closeness over chronological recency. Without time-weighted metadata or strict document deprecation rules, older documents can mathematically outrank new ones.
3. Silent Ingestion Failures
New data might be uploaded but remain unsearchable due to background parsing errors, poor chunking strategies, or asynchronous indexing delays in the vector database.
4. Context Window Limitations
Even if new data is retrieved successfully, it may get truncated if pushed beyond the LLM’s context window. Additionally, without explicit prompt instructions to prioritize recent dates, the LLM may default to its pre-trained parametric memory.
Quick Troubleshooting Step:
Intercept the raw output of your top-K retrieved chunks before they reach the LLM. This immediately clarifies whether you are facing a retrieval failure or a generation error.
