Here is how to answer it like a production AI engineer. 👇

1️⃣ Use Layout-Aware Parsing
Don’t dump raw text. Use document parsers that explicitly detect tables (Unstructured, LlamaParse, Azure Document Intelligence, AWS Textract).
👉 They preserve rows, columns, and cell boundaries instead of smashing them into a single string.

2️⃣ Keep Tables as Atomic Chunks
Never split a table mid-row.
👉 Treat each table (or logical sub-table) as a single chunk with its headers attached. This ensures "Column 3" always travels with its column name.

3️⃣ Convert Tables to Structured Text
Serialize each table into Markdown or HTML before embedding.
👉 The | Header | ... | markdown format keeps the LLM aware of the structural relationship and lets it reason perfectly about rows and columns.

4️⃣ Enrich with Metadata + Summaries
Store a natural-language summary of each table alongside it ("Quarterly revenue by region, 2021–2025").
👉 Embed the summary for clean semantic retrieval, but feed the full structured markdown table to the LLM during generation.

5️⃣ Implement Hybrid Retrieval
Combine dense semantic search with keyword/metadata filters.
👉 This ensures specific numeric and positional queries (like looking for "row 14") actually hit the right chunk.

6️⃣ For Complex Sheets ➡️ Text-to-SQL
If it’s a massive, highly tabular dataset, stop forcing it into vectors.
👉 Extract the data into a structured database and use a text-to-SQL layer. It is 10x more reliable than embedding raw numbers.

📌 Key Insight
Tables are not paragraphs. Parse for structure first, chunk second.
Paragraphs explain concepts.
Tables map relationships.
Production RAG needs both.

